// Logout permitted if the same user logged in within the last hour.
// `Login.server` is omitted from the predicate, so the runtime should
// treat it as unconstrained — the documented "omitted fields are
// implicitly wildcarded" rule. The trace deliberately writes
// `Login` / `Logout` arguments in `(user, server)` order while the
// schema's `LoginInput` / `LogoutInput` records both alphabetize to
// `(server, user)`. A driver that zips schema-ordered field names
// against trace-ordered values misaligns the two and causes the
// SINCE state to remember Login's row with `server` and `user`
// swapped, which then fails to join against the rule's
// `context.input.user`. The case asserts a `permit` verdict at the
// Logout time-point.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Logout",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
